Here's how to handle draft persistence across app lifecycle events in .NET MAUI:

**1. In your `App.xaml.cs`, hook lifecycle events:**

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    public App()
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        MainPage = new NavigationPage(new NotePage());
    }

    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState activationState)
    {
        var window = base.CreateWindow(activationState);

        window.Deactivated += (s, e) =>
        {
            // Fires when app goes to background
            MessagingCenter.Send<App>(this, "AppBackgrounded");
        };

        window.Resumed += (s, e) =>
        {
            // Fires when app returns to foreground
            MessagingCenter.Send<App>(this, "AppResumed");
        };

        return window;
    }
}
```

**2. In your `NotePage.xaml.cs`:**

```csharp
public partial class NotePage : ContentPage
{
    private const string DraftKey = "note_draft";

    public NotePage()
    {
        InitializeComponent();

        // Restore draft on page load
        RestoreDraft();

        MessagingCenter.Subscribe<App>(this, "AppBackgrounded", (sender) =>
        {
            SaveDraft();
        });
    }

    private void SaveDraft()
    {
        Preferences.Set(DraftKey, NoteEditor.Text ?? string.Empty);
    }

    private void RestoreDraft()
    {
        if (Preferences.ContainsKey(DraftKey))
        {
            NoteEditor.Text = Preferences.Get(DraftKey, string.Empty);
        }
    }

    // Clear draft when user explicitly saves/discards
    public void ClearDraft()
    {
        Preferences.Remove(DraftKey);
    }

    protected override void OnDisappearing()
    {
        base.OnDisappearing();
        MessagingCenter.Unsubscribe<App>(this, "AppBackgrounded");
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- **`Preferences`** is ideal for small text — it's synchronous and survives app termination.
- **`Window.Deactivated`** fires reliably on iOS/Android when backgrounding.
- For large drafts (>1KB), use `SecureStorage` or write to `FileSystem.AppDataDirectory` instead.
- Call `ClearDraft()` when the user taps "Save" or "Discard" so stale drafts don't reappear.

> **Note:** `MessagingCenter` is deprecated in .NET 8+. If targeting .NET 8+, use the `WeakReferenceMessenger` from the CommunityToolkit.Mvvm package instead.
